    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail before anyone writes a line of code, and they fail for a reason that has nothing to do with execution. They fail because they are treated as promises when they are actually guesses.\n\nConsider how a roadmap usually comes together. Someone in leadership wants visibility into the next four quarters. Sales wants to know what they can tell customers. Marketing needs to plan launches. Finance wants to tie headcount to output. So a product manager collects requests, sorts them into buckets labeled Q1 through Q4, and circulates a document. The moment that document exists, it stops being a hypothesis about what might create value and becomes a contract about what will ship. Every later attempt to change it looks like failure rather than learning.\n\nThe second problem is that roadmaps are almost always lists of features rather than lists of problems. A feature is a specific answer, and specific answers assume you already understand the question. Most teams do not. They have a hunch that customers are churning because onboarding is confusing, so they put \"new onboarding flow\" on the roadmap for the second quarter. Six months later the flow ships, churn does not move, and nobody revisits the assumption because the item was marked done. If the roadmap had said \"reduce first week churn\" instead, the team would have kept working the problem until something moved. The framing determines whether you are accountable for outcomes or merely for activity.\n\nThird, roadmaps ignore the cost of what is already built. Every feature shipped becomes something to maintain, support, document, and eventually migrate. Teams plan as if capacity were constant, then discover that a third of their engineering time goes to keeping the existing product alive. The roadmap made no room for this, so either the dates slip or quality quietly degrades. Both outcomes damage trust, which makes the next roadmap even more political.\n\nFourth, and this is the uncomfortable one, roadmaps often exist to manage internal anxiety rather than to direct work. A leader who cannot tolerate uncertainty asks for a plan. The plan reduces the anxiety without reducing the uncertainty. Everyone knows the fourth quarter items are fiction, but the fiction is comforting, so nobody says so. This is why so many roadmap conversations feel performative. The document is not really about the product.\n\nWhat works better is less satisfying to look at. Commit firmly to the next six or eight weeks, because you actually know something about that horizon. Describe the following quarter in terms of problems you intend to attack and the evidence that made you choose them. Beyond that, state themes and be honest that they are directional. Publish what you learned alongside what you shipped, so that changing course reads as intelligence rather than incompetence.\n\nNone of this removes the need for planning. Organizations genuinely need to coordinate, and coordination requires some shared picture of the future. The trick is building a picture that can absorb new information without shattering. A roadmap that cannot be wrong cannot be useful, because the only way to be certain about a year of software is to stop paying attention to your customers.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nI've watched more product roadmaps collapse than I can count, and it usually isn't because the ideas were bad. The real problem is that most teams treat the roadmap like a contract instead of a hypothesis. They lock in features and dates months ahead based on incomplete information, then act surprised when customers don't care, technology changes, or a competitor ships something better. Updating it feels like admitting failure, so the document slowly becomes fiction while the actual product evolves in a completely different direction.\n\nA lot of this comes from confusing outputs with outcomes. The typical roadmap is a list of things to build, not a set of problems to solve or results to achieve. Stakeholders demand their features appear on it, product managers add them to keep the peace, and suddenly the team is building a Frankenstein of requests instead of a coherent product. There's almost never enough time spent talking to users before those items get committed. Assumptions stay assumptions until it's too late and expensive to reverse.\n\nCapacity is another silent killer. Roadmaps rarely account for the messy reality of software: bugs, technical debt, onboarding, support, the unexpected production incident. Everything is estimated as if the team will operate at peak efficiency forever. When dates inevitably slip, leadership loses faith, engineering feels set up to fail, and the next planning cycle starts with even more cynicism.\n\nPolitics make it worse. The person with the most power or the loudest voice often dictates what goes on the page, regardless of data. Strategy gets diluted into a collection of unrelated bets. People also mix up a roadmap with a detailed project plan and expect precision that discovery work simply cannot provide.\n\nThe roadmaps that survive are usually shorter, more flexible, focused on themes or customer problems rather than specific features, and treated as conversation starters rather than sacred texts. Most organizations want the appearance of certainty more than they want actual learning. That's why the majority fail. They become artifacts of wishful thinking instead of tools for making better decisions.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
